---
import FAQSection from "@components/article/FAQSection.astro";
import RelatedArticles from "@components/article/RelatedArticles.astro";
import ArticleLayout from "../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro";
import { getArticleBySlug } from "../../data/articles";

// Get article metadata from central data file
const article = getArticleBySlug("secure-home-wifi-network", "guides")!;
const { title, description, authorSlug, readingTime, image, imageAlt } = article;
const pubDate = new Date(article.pubDate);

// Table of contents headings
const headings = [
  { text: "The Checklist", slug: "checklist", depth: 2 },
  { text: "The Password Situation", slug: "passwords", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Encryption Settings", slug: "encryption", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Guest Networks Save Your Bacon", slug: "guest-network", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Keeping Firmware Updated", slug: "firmware", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Features You Should Disable", slug: "disable-features", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Router-Level VPN", slug: "vpn", depth: 2 },
  { text: "For the Paranoid: Network Segmentation", slug: "segmentation", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Questions", slug: "faq", depth: 2 },
];

// FAQ data
const faqs = [
  {
    question: "How do I even get into router settings?",
    answer:
      "<p>Type 192.168.1.1 or 192.168.0.1 in your browser while connected to wifi. Some routers use 10.0.0.1. ASUS uses router. Asus. Com. Netgear uses routerlogin. Net. Default login is often admin/admin or printed on a sticker somewhere on the router.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "Should I hide my network name?",
    answer:
      "<p>Doesn't really help. Hidden networks still announce themselves. Just without the name visible. Some devices get confused and have connection problems. Waste of time. Strong encryption and good password matter. Hidden name doesn't.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "How often to check for updates?",
    answer:
      "<p>Monthly at least. Or just enable automatic updates and forget about it. Security patches matter. Vulnerabilities get discovered. Updates fix them. Monthly reminder in your calendar if automatic isn't available.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "My ISP gave me a router. Is it any good?",
    answer:
      "<p>Usually fine but not great. ISP maintains it and pushes updates. Support included. But hardware's often older. Features limited. Some ISPs have been caught with backdoors. For serious remote work, grab something like an ASUS or Netgear. Better features, better performance.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "WPA3 or stick with WPA2?",
    answer:
      "<p>WPA3 if everything supports it. Otherwise WPA2/WPA3 transitional mode gives you the best of both. Never use WEP or plain WPA. They're broken. Been broken for years.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "Do smart devices really need their own network?",
    answer:
      "<p>Kind of, yeah. Security on these things is awful. Manufacturers stop updating them. Known vulnerabilities just sit there. If something gets compromised, you want it isolated from your work laptop. Guest network handles this fine for most people.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "What router should I buy?",
    answer:
      "<p>ASUS, Netgear, TP-Link all make solid options. Look for regular firmware updates, WPA3 support, VLAN support if you want segmentation. ASUS RT-AX86U is popular. Netgear Nighthawk line is solid. TP-Link Archer series works. For mesh, eero Pro, ASUS ZenWiFi, or Google Nest WiFi Pro.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "Can neighbors steal my wifi?",
    answer:
      "<p>Not with proper security. Strong random password, twelve plus characters. WPA2 or WPA3 encryption. Uncrackable without the password. Check connected devices in your router admin panel occasionally. If wifi seems slow and you see unknown devices, kick them off and change your password.</p>",
  },
];

// Related articles
const relatedArticles = [
  {
    title: "Best VPN for Digital Nomads",
    excerpt: "Top VPN services to protect your connection anywhere.",
    href: "/tools/best-vpn-digital-nomads",
    image: "/images/unsplash/photo-1614064641938-3bbee52942c7-800w.webp",
    imageAlt: "VPN security illustration",
  },
  {
    title: "Two-Factor Authentication Guide",
    excerpt: "Set up 2FA on all your accounts for better security.",
    href: "/guides/two-factor-authentication-guide",
    image: "/images/unsplash/photo-1614064641938-3bbee52942c7-800w.webp",
    imageAlt: "Two-factor authentication",
  },
  {
    title: "Best Password Manager for Remote Work",
    excerpt: "Secure all your passwords with the right tool.",
    href: "/tools/best-password-manager-remote-work",
    image: "/images/unsplash/photo-1614064641938-3bbee52942c7-800w.webp",
    imageAlt: "Password manager interface",
  },
];
---

<ArticleLayout
    title={title}
    description={description}
    authorSlug={authorSlug}
    pubDate={pubDate}
    readingTime={readingTime}
    image={image}
    imageAlt={imageAlt}
    category="Security"
    categorySlug="guides"
    subcategorySlug="security"
    headings={headings}
>
  <p class="lead">
    So I'm setting up my home office last year and realize something uncomfortable. Same wifi network handling my kids'
    Roblox sessions, the smart TV's Netflix binges, and all my client work. Same connection. Same network. Yikes.
  </p>

  <p>
    Most home networks? Set up for convenience, not security. Default passwords nobody changed. Firmware updates ignored
    for years. Smart doorbell with zero security updates since 2019. All of these create holes attackers love finding.
  </p>

  <p>
    Took me about half an hour to fix everything. Made me sleep better. Here's how.
  </p>

  <h2 id="checklist">The Checklist</h2>
  <p>
    Everything we're hitting today. Keep this handy.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Change your router admin password. Not the wifi password, the settings password.</li>
    <li>Change your network name. NETGEAR-5G tells everyone your router brand.</li>
    <li>Strong wifi password. Twelve characters minimum, sixteen if you're paranoid like me.</li>
    <li>WPA3 or WPA2-AES encryption. Anything less is basically open.</li>
    <li>Update your firmware. When did you last check? Yeah, thought so.</li>
    <li>Kill WPS. Sounds convenient but it's a security hole.</li>
    <li>Kill remote management unless you actually use it.</li>
    <li>Guest network for all your smart home junk.</li>
    <li>Firewall on. Usually default but check anyway.</li>
    <li>Maybe VPN on the router if you want to go full paranoid.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Let's break these down.
  </p>

  <h2 id="passwords">The Password Situation</h2>
  <p>
    Two passwords matter here. Most people mix them up.
  </p>

  <h3>Your Router Admin Password</h3>
  <p>
    Gets you into settings. Where you change everything. Default is usually admin/admin or admin/password. Publicly
    known. Change it now or anyone on your network can mess with your settings.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Type 192.168.1.1 or 192.168.0.1 in your browser.</li>
    <li>Poke around for Administration or System settings.</li>
    <li>New password. Make it good. Store it in your <a href="/tools/best-password-manager-remote-work">password
      manager</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Your WiFi Password</h3>
  <p>
    What people use to join your network.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Twelve characters bare minimum. Sixteen is better.</li>
    <li>Mix it up. Letters, numbers, weird symbols.</li>
    <li>Not your dog's name. Not your address. Not your birthday.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Something like <code>purple-elephant-runs-slowly-99!</code> works. Long, weird, memorable. Use a <a
      href="/guides/do-i-really-need-password-manager">password manager</a> to generate and store these.
  </p>

  <h3>Also Change Your Network Name</h3>
  <p>
    Leaving it as NETGEAR-5G or linksys tells attackers exactly what hardware you're running. Makes targeting easier.
    Change it to something that doesn't identify you or your router.
  </p>

  <h2 id="encryption">Encryption Settings</h2>
  <p>
    Stops neighbors and randos from snooping on your traffic.
  </p>

  <h3>WPA3 Is the Good Stuff</h3>
  <p>
    <a href="https://www.wi-fi.org/discover-wi-fi/security" target="_blank" rel="nofollow">Newest standard</a> from the
    Wi-Fi Alliance. Out since 2018 but lots of routers still don't have it. Harder to crack. Protects against password
    guessing attacks. Even if someone gets your password later, they can't decrypt old traffic. Neat trick called
    forward secrecy.
  </p>
  <p>
    Go WPA3 if your router and devices all support it.
  </p>

  <h3>WPA2-Personal Is Fine Too</h3>
  <p>
    Been around longer. Still solid with a good password. Works with basically every device out there. <a
      href="https://csrc.nist.gov/projects/cryptographic-standards-and-guidelines/archived-crypto-projects/aes-development"
      target="_blank" rel="nofollow">AES encryption
    underneath</a> per NIST standards. Nobody's cracked it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Use this if older devices choke on WPA3.
  </p>

  <h3>The Hybrid Option</h3>
  <p>
    WPA2/WPA3 transitional mode. WPA3 for devices that can handle it, WPA2 for the rest. Keeps compatibility high while
    maxing out security where possible. Probably the best default for most homes.
  </p>

  <h3>Stay Away From</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>WEP:</strong> Ancient. <a
        href="https://www.nist.gov/news-events/news/2001/11/nist-issues-draft-fips-wlan-security" target="_blank"
        rel="nofollow">Cracked in minutes</a> according to security researchers.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Plain WPA:</strong> Also broken.</li>
    <li><strong>Open networks:</strong> Why even have a password?</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="guest-network">Guest Networks Save Your Bacon</h2>
  <p>
    Separate network. Isolated from your main one. Two reasons this matters.
  </p>

  <h3>When People Visit</h3>
  <p>
    Give guests the guest network password. They get internet but can't poke around your shared drives or computers.
    Their infected laptop stays contained. Doesn't spread to your stuff. Don't have to change your main password after
    they leave.
  </p>

  <h3>All Your Smart Home Gadgets</h3>
  <p>
    Your smart TV. The Ring doorbell. Those cheap smart bulbs. Security cameras. These things often have terrible
    security. Updates stop after a year. Known vulnerabilities just sit there. Put them all on the guest network. They
    can still do their job. Can't reach your work laptop though. If something gets owned, the damage stays isolated.
  </p>

  <h3>Setting It Up</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Open router settings.</li>
    <li>Hunt for Guest Network or Guest WiFi.</li>
    <li>Turn it on. Give it a different password.</li>
    <li>Look for AP Isolation or Client Isolation. Turn that on too.</li>
    <li>Maybe cap the bandwidth so guests don't kill your connection.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="firmware">Keeping Firmware Updated</h2>
  <p>
    Router firmware is just software. Needs patches like anything else. Here's the thing. Most people never update it.
    Ever.
  </p>

  <h3>Why You Should Care</h3>
  <p>
    Security holes get discovered. Firmware patches fix them. Sometimes you get stability improvements too. Occasionally
    they even add new features. Like WPA3 support on older routers.
  </p>

  <h3>Actually Doing It</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Open router admin panel.</li>
    <li>Hunt around for Firmware Update or Software Update.</li>
    <li>Hit Check for Updates.</li>
    <li>If something's available, install it.</li>
    <li>Wait for restart. Keep it plugged in. Unplugging mid-update can brick your router.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Set It and Forget It</h3>
  <p>
    Modern routers often do automatic updates. Turn that on if you can.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>ASUS</strong> has it under Administration, then Firmware Upgrade.</li>
    <li><strong>Netgear</strong> puts it in Advanced, then Administration.</li>
    <li><strong>TP-Link</strong> buries it in System Tools.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If your router doesn't do automatic updates, stick a monthly reminder in your calendar. Seriously. Just do it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="disable-features">Features You Should Disable</h2>
  <p>
    Some stuff sounds helpful but creates security holes.
  </p>

  <h3>WPS</h3>
  <p>
    The button that lets you connect devices without typing a password. Or an eight digit PIN. Problem? <a
      href="https://www.us-cert.gov/ncas/alerts/TA12-006A" target="_blank" rel="nofollow">That PIN can be
    guessed by a computer trying combinations</a> as US-CERT warned. Takes hours, not years. Find it in your router
    settings. Disable it. Just
    use your actual password.
  </p>

  <h3>Remote Access</h3>
  <p>
    Lets you manage your router from anywhere in the world. Most people don't need this. Creates an attack point though.
    Turn off Remote Management and Remote Administration. Same with Cloud Management unless you specifically want it.
    Need remote access? <a href="/tools/best-vpn-digital-nomads">VPN</a> into your home network instead. Much safer.
  </p>

  <h3>UPnP</h3>
  <p>
    Universal Plug and Play. Lets devices poke holes in your firewall automatically. Convenient for gaming and streaming
    devices. <a href="https://www.us-cert.gov/ncas/alerts/TA13-175A" target="_blank" rel="nofollow">Also lets malware
    poke holes in your firewall automatically</a> according to security researchers. Less convenient. Disable it unless
    something
    breaks. Then you know what actually needs it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="vpn">Router-Level VPN</h2>
  <p>
    Put a VPN on your router itself. Now everything on your network is protected. No apps to install on individual
    devices.
  </p>

  <h3>Why That's Cool</h3>
  <p>
    Every device covered. Even stuff that can't run VPN apps. One connection to manage instead of five. Smart TV, gaming
    console, that weird IoT thing. All protected.
  </p>

  <h3>Tradeoffs</h3>
  <p>
    Everything goes through the VPN. Might slow things down. Not every router can do this. Need specific models or
    custom firmware. Netflix and friends sometimes block VPN connections. Annoying.
  </p>

  <h3>Making It Happen</h3>
  <p>
    Check if your router supports VPN client mode. ASUS routers usually do. Netgear too. DD-WRT firmware adds it to
    others. Grab OpenVPN or WireGuard config files from your VPN provider. They usually have instructions. Router
    settings, find VPN, then VPN Client. Upload the config. Enable it. Test it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href="/tools/best-vpn-digital-nomads">NordVPN, ExpressVPN, Surfshark</a>, PIA all have decent router support.
  </p>

  <h2 id="segmentation">For the Paranoid: Network Segmentation</h2>
  <p>
    Want to go further? Split your network into separate zones.
  </p>

  <h3>Why Bother</h3>
  <p>
    Something gets compromised? Stays in its zone. Work stuff can't see personal stuff and vice versa. Some employers
    actually require this.
  </p>

  <h3>VLAN Setup</h3>
  <p>
    VLANs let you run multiple logical networks on one physical network.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Work VLAN:</strong> Work laptop and work phone.</li>
    <li><strong>Personal VLAN:</strong> Personal devices.</li>
    <li><strong>IoT VLAN:</strong> All the smart home stuff.</li>
    <li><strong>Guest VLAN:</strong> Visitors.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>What You Actually Need</h3>
  <p>
    Router that supports VLANs. Many prosumer ones do. Managed switch if you want wired separation.time to figure it
    out.
  </p>
  <p>
    Honestly? For most people, just putting IoT stuff on a guest network gets you most of the benefit. Less complexity.
  </p>

  <h2 id="faq">Questions</h2>

  <FAQSection faqs={faqs}/>

  <h2>Bottom Line</h2>
  <p>
    Half an hour of work. Real reduction in risk. Your home network is your office network now. Treat it that way.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Start today with passwords and encryption.</li>
    <li>This week tackle firmware and WPS.</li>
    <li>This month consider VPN on router or guest network for IoT.</li>
    <li>Monthly calendar reminder for firmware checks.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Foundation's important. Build it right.
  </p>

  <!-- Related Articles -->
  <Fragment slot="full-width">
    <RelatedArticles articles={relatedArticles}/>
  </Fragment>
</ArticleLayout>
